<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Shaki Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1743 CE – 1819 CE · capital Nukha</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A khanate in the silk country of north-western Azerbaijan, founded in a revolt against Nader Shah and known for the painted Palace of the Shaki Khans.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1743 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajji-chelebi">Hajji Chelebi</a>, a local notable, led a rising against the heavy taxes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a> and defeated the forces sent against him. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaki-azerbaijan">Shaki</a> an independent khanate, one of the first of the Caucasian khanates, and after a mudflow destroyed old Shaki the capital moved to Nukha.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The khans ruled a land rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, orchards and crafts, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaki-azerbaijan">Nukha</a> became a major centre of silk weaving. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajji-chelebi">Hajji Chelebi</a> fought the Georgian kings and neighbouring khans. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-the-shaki-khans">Palace of the Shaki Khans</a>, built in the late 18th century, is famous for its painted interiors and stained-glass shebeke windows.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Weakened by family feuds, the khanate accepted Russian protection in 1805. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-khan-of-shaki">Ismail Khan</a> in 1819, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> abolished it and created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaki-azerbaijan">Shaki</a> province. The palace survives and, with the historic centre of Shaki, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/shaki-khanate.jpg" alt="Shaki Khanate" width="320" height="267"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banner_of_Sheki_khan_Selim,_captured_in_1806.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1743 CE – 1819 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nukha</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hajji Chelebi, Muhammad Hasan Khan, Ismail Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Azerbaijani</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sheki Khanate, Khanate of Shaki</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1781&amp;focus=shaki-khanate" aria-label="Shaki Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5833 708 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Shaki Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6153 964L6298 1144M6153 964L6487 457"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6298 1144h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6153 964h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6153 964h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1781&amp;focus=shaki-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
